Your role is licensable under the Private Security Industry Act 2001 and needs a valid SIA licence ({{job.licence}}). You must:

- keep that licence valid throughout your employment and wear or display it on duty where the law requires;
- tell us straight away if your licence is suspended, revoked or made subject to conditions, or if you are charged with or convicted of any offence;
- apply to renew it in good time before it expires.

You must not carry out licensable work without a valid licence. If you lose your licence, we will consider whether other suitable work is available. If none is, your employment may end.

The Company or the client may issue you with equipment and access items, for example keys, access cards, alarm codes, radios, phones and body-worn cameras. Each item is recorded when it is issued to you, and you may be asked to sign for it.

You must:

- use equipment only for your work and as you have been trained and instructed;
- keep keys, cards and codes secure, never copy them or share them with anyone not authorised to have them, and never take them away from the site unless you are told to;
- check equipment at the start of your shift and report any fault straight away;
- report any loss, theft or damage to the control room or your supervisor straight away, so that locks, codes or access can be changed.

All equipment and access items remain the property of the Company or the client. You must return them when asked, when you stop working at the site and when your employment ends.

The Company will only deduct the cost of lost or damaged equipment from your pay as allowed by the clause on deductions from pay.

The Supplier must keep confidential, and make sure Supplier Personnel keep confidential, all information it receives through the services, including:

- information about the Company's clients, their sites, security arrangements, alarm codes, keys and procedures;
- information about residents, visitors, customers and members of the public at those sites;
- information about the Company's staff, rotas, rates, methods and business;
- operational information such as incident reports, CCTV and body-worn video, patrol records and assignment instructions.

The Supplier may only use this information to provide the services, and may only share it with Supplier Personnel who need it for that purpose and are bound by the same duty. These duties continue after this agreement ends. On request, and when the agreement ends, the Supplier must return or securely destroy the information and confirm in writing that it has done so.

These duties do not apply to information that is already public through no fault of the Supplier, or that the law, a court or a regulator requires to be disclosed. Nothing in this agreement prevents anyone from making a protected disclosure or reporting a crime.
